  Info.plist for the gonomadnet appliance.

  Every usage description here is written for the reader who sees it, because iOS shows
  these strings in a system alert and a reader who does not understand why an app wants
  their location has no way to find out afterwards. Two of them are deliberately about
  what the appliance does NOT do, because a Reticulum node's position is the most
  sensitive thing it handles and "we never send it anywhere you did not ask for" is the
  answer to the question the alert is really asking.

  The version keys are substituted by the build from nomadnet/version/version.go, so a
  release's version is written in one place. See scripts/build-ios-app.sh.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>CFBundleDevelopmentRegion</key>
	<string>en</string>
	<key>CFBundleDisplayName</key>
	<string>gonomadnet</string>
	<key>CFBundleExecutable</key>
	<string>$(EXECUTABLE_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundleIdentifier</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_BUNDLE_IDENTIFIER)</string>
	<key>CFBundleInfoDictionaryVersion</key>
	<string>6.0</string>
	<key>CFBundleName</key>
	<string>$(PRODUCT_NAME)</string>
	<key>CFBundlePackageType</key>
	<string>APPL</string>
	<key>CFBundleShortVersionString</key>
	<string>$(GONOMADNET_VERSION)</string>
	<key>CFBundleVersion</key>
	<string>$(GONOMADNET_BUILD)</string>
	<key>LSRequiresIPhoneOS</key>
	<true/>
	<key>UIApplicationSceneManifest</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIApplicationSupportsMultipleScenes</key>
		<false/>
	</dict>
	<key>UILaunchScreen</key>
	<dict>
		<key>UIColorName</key>
		<string>ApplianceBackground</string>
	</dict>
	<key>UIRequiredDeviceCapabilities</key>
	<array>
		<string>arm64</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>
	<key>UISupportedInterfaceOrientations~ipad</key>
	<array>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortrait</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationPortraitUpsideDown</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeLeft</string>
		<string>UIInterfaceOrientationLandscapeRight</string>
	</array>

	<!-- The console font: the Nerd Font the client's glyph set is drawn in, and the
	     license it ships under. Without the font the client's interface renders as boxes
	     where it means to draw icons. -->
	<key>UIAppFonts</key>
	<array>
		<string>AtkynsonMonoNerdFontMono-Regular.otf</string>
	</array>

	<!-- The appliance's own position. It is published to a loopback socket on the device
	     and nowhere else: the reader sends it where they choose to send it. -->
	<key>NSLocationWhenInUseUsageDescription</key>
	<string>gonomadnet uses your position to show where you are on the pages and messages that ask, and to answer the bot's "where" command. It is published only to the appliance on this device; nothing is sent anywhere unless you send it.</string>

	<!-- The camera, for reading an address out of a QR code instead of having it typed. The
	     string is the whole of what a reader is told when the system asks, so it says what the
	     camera is for and what happens to the pictures: they are decoded on the device to find
	     a code and thrown away, and nothing in them is kept or sent anywhere. -->
	<key>NSCameraUsageDescription</key>
	<string>gonomadnet reads a QR code with your camera when you press Scan beside an address field, so the address does not have to be typed. Each frame is decoded on this device to find a code and discarded immediately; nothing is saved, and nothing is sent anywhere.</string>

	<!-- CoreMotion's attitude sensor, which is what the compass is. -->
	<key>NSMotionUsageDescription</key>
	<string>gonomadnet reads the device's orientation to give the client a compass heading.</string>

	<!-- Bluetooth, for a LoRa radio. -->
	<key>NSBluetoothAlwaysUsageDescription</key>
	<string>gonomadnet connects to an RNode over Bluetooth to send and receive Reticulum traffic with a LoRa radio.</string>
	<key>NSBluetoothPeripheralUsageDescription</key>
	<string>gonomadnet connects to an RNode over Bluetooth to send and receive Reticulum traffic with a LoRa radio.</string>
</dict>
</plist>
